\title{BreastCancerFNA: Predict Breast Mass Malignancy from Fine Needle Aspirate Nuclear Features}
\description{
Predicts whether a breast mass is malignant (\code{"M"}) or benign
(\code{"B"}) from the 30 cell-nucleus features of the Breast Cancer
Wisconsin (Diagnostic) Data Set. The features describe nuclei in a
digitised image of a fine needle aspirate (FNA): ten measurements (radius,
texture, perimeter, area, smoothness, compactness, concavity, concave
points, symmetry and fractal dimension), each summarised as \code{_mean},
\code{_se} (standard error) and \code{_worst}.
}
\section{Functions}{

\describe{
  \item{\code{\link{predict_diagnosis}}}{Returns class labels
    \code{"M"} / \code{"B"} (default threshold 0.19).}
  \item{\code{\link{predict_malignancy_probability}}}{Returns the predicted
    probability of malignancy (0 to 1).}
}
}

\section{Model}{

A multilayer perceptron (\code{nnet}, 5 hidden units, weight decay 0.001)
selected by nested cross-validation (5 outer x 5 inner folds) among logistic
regression, random forest, XGBoost and the MLP. Pooled held-out performance:
AUROC 0.992, AUPRC 0.991. The decision threshold 0.19 maximises the F2
score (recall weighted twice as much as precision).
}

\section{Example data}{

Ten example samples are stored in
\code{system.file("extdata", "example_input.csv", package = "BreastCancerFNA")}.
Read them with \code{check.names = FALSE} to keep column names such as
\code{concave points_mean}.
}

